from common import lesson, lab, cite

LESSONS = [
lesson(12,4,'sae-features','Features from sparse autoencoders, and how their explanations are scored',
 'What an SAE feature is, how Neuronpedia presents one, what its explanation score means, and why the 2026 debate is partly about whether to train the second model at all.',
 '''<h2 id="what-an-sae-is">The second model</h2>
<p>A sparse autoencoder is trained to reconstruct a layer’s activations through a bottleneck that is wider than the layer but sparse: an encoder maps the activation vector to many latents of which only a few are active, and a decoder maps those back. Each latent is a direction in activation space plus a sparse activation, and the claim is that these directions are a better unit of analysis than neurons. Anthropic’s 2023 paper did this on “a one-layer transformer with a 512-neuron MLP layer” and reported that “Sparse autoencoders produce interpretable features that are effectively invisible in the neuron basis. We find features (e.g., one firing on Hebrew script) which are not active in any of the top dataset examples for any of the neurons” '''+cite('monosemantic')+'''. Their blinded human rubric gave the median neuron a 0 (“our annotator could not even form a hypothesis of what the neuron could represent!”) and the median feature a 12.</p>
<p>OpenAI’s 2024 paper scaled the method with a k-sparse (TopK) activation that fixes the number of active latents directly, trained “a 16 million latent autoencoder on GPT-4 activations for 40 billion tokens,” and proposed quality metrics beyond reconstruction: “whether certain hypothesized features were recovered, whether downstream effects are sparse, and whether features can be explained with both high precision and recall” '''+cite('gao2024')+'''. One of those is downstream loss: “How good is the language model loss if the residual stream latent is replaced with the autoencoder reconstruction of that latent?” For their largest autoencoder, substituting it into GPT-4 gave a loss “corresponding to 10% of the pretraining compute of GPT-4,” which is a candid way to report how much the reconstruction loses.</p>
<h2 id="feature-as-claim">A feature is a claim like any other</h2>
<p>For the purposes of this course, an SAE feature is a direction chosen by an unsupervised search, and its explanation is scored exactly the way a neuron’s is: a simulator (model or rule) predicts the feature’s activation from the explanation alone on held-out tokens, and the prediction is scored against the truth. The 2023 paper used a computational proxy for one of its features, Arabic script, and reports “a Pearson correlation of 0.74 between the activity of our feature and the activity of the Arabic script proxy (thresholded at 0), over a dataset of 40 million tokens” '''+cite('monosemantic')+'''. That is lesson 04’s score with a rule as the simulator, at scale. Everything lesson 05 said about controls applies: a random feature scored against the same proxy, the proxy shuffled, and a baseline explanation.</p>
<p>Two further tests exist for features that do not exist for neurons, because a feature comes with a reconstruction. The reconstruction error says how much of the layer the whole dictionary explains; the downstream loss says how much the model cares. A feature can have a clean explanation and belong to a dictionary that wrecks the model when spliced in, and then the explanation is of something the model does not quite compute.</p>
<h2 id="neuronpedia">Looking one up</h2>
<p>Neuronpedia describes itself as “an open source interpretability platform” and hosts, among other releases, residual-stream sparse autoencoders for GPT-2 small, with a search over latents by explanation text or by running custom text through a model '''+cite('neuronpedia')+'''. A feature page typically shows its top-activating examples, an auto-generated explanation, that explanation’s score, activation histograms, and the tokens it promotes and suppresses. Reading such a page with this course’s habits:</p>
<table><tr><th>Element on the page</th><th>What it is</th><th>What to ask</th></tr>
<tr><td>Top activating examples</td><td>Lesson 03’s top-k view</td><td>Where are the quantile bands?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explanation</td><td>A hypothesis written from the top examples</td><td>By whom, from how many examples?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explanation score</td><td>Usually a correlation on some excerpt mix</td><td>Which excerpts: top-and-random, or all tokens?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Activation histogram</td><td>The distribution the explanation must cover</td><td>How sparse? What fraction is weak?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Logit effects</td><td>Direct attribution of the feature direction</td><td>Direct path only; patched?</td></tr></table>
<p>A network lookup is optional for this course and nothing in the labs depends on it. The exercise is to read the page as a protocol with fields filled in by someone else, and to notice which fields are empty.</p>
<h2 id="the-debate">Second model or native neurons</h2>
<p>The 2026 essay’s central methodological claim is that the second model may be unnecessary and sometimes harmful. It describes two approaches, “rapid normal science on the network itself” versus starting “from a list of hypothesized features” and training a decomposition, and argues the field “fell back to the second because neural networks seemed to be tangled knots” '''+cite('cammarata2026')+'''. Its vision experiment retrains InceptionV1 with mixed3b squeezed to 10 channels and trains an SAE on the squeezed layer: “We don’t see anything like clean curve neurons!” It cites Gorton’s SAE study of InceptionV1 early vision '''+cite('gorton2024')+''' and argues the SAE curves there “decompose into existing curve-responsive neurons.” And it reports, for Qwen3-32B, “roughly 90% of neuron poles have one main job, often with small related offshoots; another ~7% have two related jobs; and only ~2-4% are not yet understood,” with the caveat that the number depends on thresholds for weak activations and on “what you view as ‘one thing’.”</p>
<p>Gao’s reply accepts the observation and keeps superposition as the explanation for what circuits look like: “My current best explanation for this is that concepts are stored in superposition, and MLPs do computation on those features in superposition, by doing more than one computation per neuron” '''+cite('gao2026')+'''. The two positions make different predictions that the methods in this course can test on a small model: if native neurons are clean, a rule-form explanation of a neuron should reach a high held-out score and substituting the simulated activations should barely change the model; if features are in superposition, SAE latents should score higher than the best neuron on the same proxy, and splicing the dictionary in should hurt less than ablating the neuron. Neither side disputes that the test is the right arbiter. That is the course’s position too, and it is why the capstone scores a native neuron rather than a feature: it is the cheaper experiment and the one the essay invites.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Treat an SAE feature’s explanation score as you would a neuron’s from Bills et al.: read which excerpts it was computed on, and remember Huang et al.’s result that a high top-and-random score is compatible with an F1 of 0.56 in a full observational audit. The platform tells you the score; the protocol tells you what it is a score of.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>“Feature splitting” read as discovery. The 2023 paper reports that “Features appear to ‘split’ as we increase autoencoder size,” one base64 feature becoming three. Whether three features are more true than one is a question about the model, not about the autoencoder, and the essay’s complaint about SAEs that “fracture” neurons is the same question from the other side. A split that improves held-out prediction of the model’s behaviour is evidence; one that only improves the dictionary’s reconstruction is not.</p></div>''',
 'A feature page shows an explanation “mentions of rivers” with score 0.83 and a histogram where 95% of nonzero activations are below a tenth of the maximum. What does the score probably reflect, and what would you run?',
 'With that histogram, a top-and-random score of 0.83 almost certainly reflects the top excerpts, where river mentions dominate, and says little about the weak 95%. Run lesson 04’s procedure: a rule for “river mention” scored on all tokens of a held-out text, with a random-feature control and the shuffle. If the all-token score is far below 0.83, the explanation covers the feature’s strong tail only, and the write-up should say which regime it explains.',
 lab([], 'Without a network, write the protocol (Appendix A) for testing one claimed SAE feature of your choice from a paper, as if you had the dictionary weights. Name the proxy rule, the held-out data, the two controls, the reconstruction check, and the downstream-loss check. If you have a network, open one GPT-2 small feature on Neuronpedia and fill the protocol from its page, marking every field the page leaves empty.',
 'The field most often empty is the control: feature pages report a score but rarely what a random feature scores on the same explanation. The second is the excerpt mix the score used. A protocol with those two fields filled would let you compare the feature against a native neuron found by lesson 05’s screening on the same rule, which is the experiment the 2026 debate is asking for.'), ['monosemantic','gao2024','neuronpedia','cammarata2026','gao2026','gorton2024','huang2023']),

lesson(13,4,'writing-it-up','Writing it up: the demonstrated-explanation checklist and the Gao critique',
 'How to report a result so that it is a contribution, how to report a failure so that it is one too, and the exact shape of the 2026 disagreement about simulation scores.',
 '''<h2 id="checklist">The checklist</h2>
<p>A write-up of an explanation is complete when a reader can answer every row without asking you.</p>
<table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr>
<tr><td>What is the unit, exactly (model, weights, layer, hook, index)?</td><td>Lesson 02</td></tr>
<tr><td>How was it chosen, and how many candidates were searched?</td><td>Lessons 01, 05</td></tr>
<tr><td>What is the claim as a prediction, and what data is it scored on?</td><td>Lesson 01</td></tr>
<tr><td>Was the held-out data really untouched before the score?</td><td>Lesson 01 (the “looked?” field)</td></tr>
<tr><td>What is the score, what is its base rate, and what did each control score?</td><td>Lessons 04, 05</td></tr>
<tr><td>Was the threshold fixed before the run, and did it respect the base rate?</td><td>Lesson 08</td></tr>
<tr><td>Which regime of the activation distribution does the score cover?</td><td>Lessons 03, 04</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is the evidence correlational or interventional, and is the claim worded to match?</td><td>Lesson 10</td></tr>
<tr><td>What was not tested, in a table?</td><td>Lesson 07</td></tr>
<tr><td>If the test failed, is the failure reported with the same detail as a pass would be?</td><td>This lesson</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="negative">Reporting a negative result</h2>
<p>This course contains two. The named-unit vision test (lesson 06) failed because the units named in the paper do not exist in these weights; the probe test (lesson 08) failed because the threshold ignored the base rate. Each is reported with its full table, the pre-registered verdict, and the diagnosis. Neither is softened into “partial success.” The template for a negative result is the same as for a positive one with two additions: the diagnosis (what, specifically, the failure is evidence of: a wrong model, a wrong threshold, a wrong claim) and the revised protocol you would run next, labelled as not yet run.</p>
<p>The reason to be strict about this is in Nanda’s guide: “The default state of the world is that your research is false, because doing research is hard,” and “Excitement is evidence of bullshit: Generally, most true results are not exciting, but a fair amount of false results are” '''+cite('nanda')+'''. A field in which negative results are reported is one where positive results can be believed. A blog post reports what worked; a contribution reports what was tested.</p>
<h2 id="gao">The Gao critique, exactly</h2>
<p>Gao’s post makes three moves, and a write-up should be able to say which of them it answers.</p>
<p><strong>The pattern in the examples is not the explanation.</strong> “Even if there is a simple pattern that matches the activating examples of a neuron, that still doesn’t mean that the explanation is correct,” for two reasons: the pattern may not be sufficient (“perhaps not all examples that fit the pattern also activate the neuron”), and “there might actually be really important information in the activation magnitudes” '''+cite('gao2026')+'''. Lessons 03 and 04 are the response: score on all held-out tokens, report recall as well as precision, and say which regime the score covers.</p>
<p><strong>Simulation, with the right score.</strong> “If you can’t simulate the neuron in a way that doesn’t degrade the model, you don’t really understand the neuron.” The procedure is a rubric that assigns a simulated value to each input, “substituting neuron values with the simulated values,” and the test is downstream: “If this process heavily degrades the model, then our explanation was wrong.” Gao argues that the objections to simulation scoring are objections to the original correlation version, “fixed basically entirely by (a) measuring downstream KL instead and (b) scoring on a large dataset.” The course’s correlation score is the original version; the KL variant is the natural next step and the capstone asks you to describe how you would run it.</p>
<p><strong>The field’s failure mode.</strong> “Historically, the field of interpretability has often gotten stuck because of a lack of rigor, focusing on things that look cool even when those things oversell our actual level of understanding.”</p>
<h2 id="cammarata">Cammarata’s reply in advance</h2>
<p>The essay has a section titled “On Simulation Scores” that anticipates the second move. Its argument is that “Simulation scores often end up measuring where attention puts information, which is a question about attention motifs rather than about the neuron,” that “Simulation also rewards fine details of weak activations that matter little for understanding what a neuron does,” and that “language models are poor at outputting precise activation values even when they understand what’s going on.” Its conclusion: “So I’d rather rely on normal science than on any one metric. Simulation might eventually become one useful score among several, but later, once we understand networks well, and probably with simulators trained for the job. But I don’t think they’re a good place to start” '''+cite('cammarata2026')+'''. Elsewhere it says the 2023 metrics were “rewarding explanations that could simulate activations rather than ones that were right, which often come apart,” and that it no longer thinks the activation-reconstruction task “is a great metric for explanations.”</p>
<p>Read the two side by side and the disagreement is narrow and empirical. Both agree that top examples are insufficient. Both agree that predictions from the explanation alone, on new inputs, are the test (the essay’s agent-written positive controls and “distant concept” texts are such predictions, with their own numbers: the distant texts “fired their neurons strongly about 1% of the time, and 179 of the 183 neurons never fired on them at all”). They disagree about whether a numeric simulation score, and specifically one that penalises errors in weak activations, is the right arbiter now. Gao’s KL proposal is designed to answer the essay’s objection directly: if weak activations and attention-placed information do not matter to the output, KL will not punish getting them wrong. Whether that is true is a measurement nobody in this course has run; the capstone’s last section asks you to design it.</p>
<h2 id="blog-vs-contribution">What separates a blog post from a contribution</h2>
<p>Not the length, the venue, or the model size. A blog post can be a contribution and a paper can be a blog post. The difference is whether the reader can tell what would have happened if the claim were false. A contribution states the test before the result, scores on data the claim did not touch, shows the controls, fixes the threshold first, and reports failures at the same resolution as successes. The 2026 essay calls itself “an informal research essay and working hypothesis” and says “A technical companion paper will offer a more detailed account”; the critique says “I have a post on this in the works” and “Hopefully I'll be able to share something more concrete soon.” Both are telling you where they are on this scale. Do the same.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Put the protocol, verbatim and dated, at the top of the write-up, followed by the results table, followed by what was not tested. Anything you learned after the run goes in a section labelled exploratory. This order is unnatural to write and natural to read.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reporting the control as a footnote. The control rate belongs in the same sentence as the pass rate; lesson 07’s “12/12 against 22/100” is one number, not two.</p></div>''',
 'A draft says: “Neuron N is a date neuron (explanation score 0.81). Ablating it changed the loss by 0.002 nats, so it is not important.” Name two problems.',
 'First, the score’s data is unstated: 0.81 on which excerpts, against what control, with what base rate. Second, the ablation result is read backwards: 0.002 nats for a single neuron among 36,864 may be large or small, and Gao’s footnote notes that “Each individual neuron has a very small effect on the entire network, so we’d expect the number to be very small in some absolute sense”; compare against random neurons’ ablation effects before calling it unimportant. A third, if you want it: “date neuron” has become a noun phrase, which is how a hypothesis turns into a fact in the retelling.',
 lab([], 'Take any one result from this course’s transcript and write it up in the order the lesson gives: protocol, results table with controls, not-tested table, exploratory section. Then write the same result as a two-sentence blog-post claim, and list what the second version lets a reader get wrong.',
 'The two-sentence version almost always drops the control and the held-out qualifier, and often promotes the unit to a noun (“the curve detector,” “the induction head”). The full version is longer and less quotable; it is the one a replicator can use.'), ['gao2026','cammarata2026','nanda','bills2023','huang2023']),
]
